# Vapi Voice Agent Setup Guide for Mock Interview App

## How It Works — Architecture

Your codebase already has 90% of the integration built. Here's how the pieces connect:

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant U as User (Browser)
    participant App as Next.js App
    participant Vapi as Vapi Cloud
    participant Gemini as Gemini API

    Note over App: Step 1: User creates interview
    U->>App: Select field + level → Start Interview
    App->>Gemini: Generate interview questions
    Gemini-->>App: Returns questions array
    App-->>U: Redirect to /interview/[id]

    Note over U: Step 2: User clicks "Start Interview" voice button
    U->>Vapi: vapi.start(assistantId, overrides)
    Note over Vapi: Vapi uses your assistant config<br/>+ runtime system prompt override<br/>with the generated questions

    Vapi-->>U: Voice call starts (WebRTC)
    U->>Vapi: Candidate speaks answers
    Vapi-->>U: AI interviewer asks next question
    Vapi-->>App: Transcript events (message)

    Note over U: Step 3: Call ends
    Vapi-->>App: call-end event
    App->>Gemini: Send transcript for feedback
    Gemini-->>App: Score + feedback JSON
    App-->>U: Show results
```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> Your code already has `useVapiInterview.ts`, `VoiceInterviewPanel.tsx`, and `vapiConfig.ts` ready. You just need to **create the assistant on Vapi's dashboard** and **paste the Assistant ID** into your `.env.local`.

---

## Step 1: Create a Vapi Account

1. Go to **[dashboard.vapi.ai](https://dashboard.vapi.ai/)**
2. Click **Sign Up** (you can use Google/GitHub)
3. You'll land on the Vapi Dashboard

> [!TIP]
> Vapi gives you **free credits** when you sign up — enough to test the voice interview flow multiple times.

---

## Step 2: Get Your Public API Key

1. In the dashboard, click **⚙️ Organization Settings** (bottom-left sidebar)
2. Go to the **API Keys** section
3. Copy your **Public Key** — it starts with something like `e947e935-...`

> [!NOTE]  
> You already have this in your `.env.local` as `NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPI_WEB_TOKEN=e947e935-92b4-4eb8-8dbf-d3ffc3ea9a1a`. If this is still valid, you can skip this step. If it's expired or wrong, replace it.

---

## Step 3: Create the Mock Interviewer Assistant

This is the main step. You're creating the "brain" of the voice agent.

### 3.1 — Open the Assistants Page

1. In the dashboard sidebar, click **Assistants**
2. Click **+ Create Assistant**
3. Choose **"Blank"** template (not any pre-made one)

### 3.2 — Configure the Model

In the **Model** section:

| Setting | Value |
|---------|-------|
| **Provider** | `Google` |
| **Model** | `gemini-2.0-flash` (or `gemini-1.5-flash`) |
| **Temperature** | `0.7` |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> Your code overrides the system prompt at runtime (see `useVapiInterview.ts` line 67-73). The dashboard system prompt is just a fallback. But set a reasonable default anyway.

### 3.3 — Set the System Prompt (Default/Fallback)

Paste this into the **System Prompt** field:

```
You are a professional mock interviewer. You conduct technical interviews in a professional, supportive manner.

Behavior:
- Greet the candidate briefly, state the role and interview type, then start asking questions.
- After each answer, give a short acknowledgment (one sentence), then move to the next question.
- Keep your spoken turns concise.
- End professionally after the last question: thank them and say the session is complete.

Tone: supportive, neutral, interview-realistic.
```

### 3.4 — Set the First Message

In the **First Message** field, enter:

```
Hi there! I'm your mock interviewer. We'll get started shortly — let me set up the session.
```

> [!NOTE]
> This first message is also overridden at runtime by your code (`useVapiInterview.ts` line 68) with a message specific to the interview role/level. So this is just a fallback.

### 3.5 — Configure the Voice

In the **Voice** section:

| Setting | Value |
|---------|-------|
| **Provider** | `11labs` (ElevenLabs) or `Vapi` (default) |
| **Voice** | Pick any professional-sounding voice. Recommended: `Rachel` or `Adam` from ElevenLabs, or the default Vapi voice |

> [!TIP]
> The default Vapi voice works fine for testing. You can upgrade to ElevenLabs later for more natural sound.

### 3.6 — Configure the Transcriber

In the **Transcriber** section:

| Setting | Value |
|---------|-------|
| **Provider** | `Deepgram` (default) |
| **Model** | `nova-2` |
| **Language** | `en` |

### 3.7 — Save / Publish

Click **Publish** (or **Save**) at the top-right.

### 3.8 — Copy the Assistant ID

After saving:
1. The URL will look like: `dashboard.vapi.ai/assistants/abc12345-...`
2. Copy the assistant ID from the URL, OR
3. Click the **⋯ menu** → **Copy ID**

The ID looks like: `abc12345-6789-4def-abcd-1234567890ab`

---

## Step 4: Update Your `.env.local`

Open your `.env.local` file and replace the placeholder:

```diff
 NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPI_WEB_TOKEN=e947e935-92b4-4eb8-8dbf-d3ffc3ea9a1a
-NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPI_ASSISTANT_ID=your_vapi_assistant_id_here
+NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPI_ASSISTANT_ID=<paste-your-real-assistant-id-here>
```

> [!WARNING]
> After changing `.env.local`, you **must restart the dev server** (`Ctrl+C` then `npm run dev`).

---

## Step 5: Test the Voice Interview

1. Go to your app → create a new interview (select field + level)
2. You'll land on the interview page `/interview/[id]`
3. You should see the **"Voice interview (Vapi)"** card with:
   - **Start interview** button (should be enabled now)
   - **End & get feedback** button
4. Click **Start interview**
5. Allow microphone access when the browser asks
6. The AI interviewer will greet you and start asking questions
7. Answer the questions, then click **End & get feedback**
8. Wait for Gemini to analyze the transcript and show your score

---

## How the Runtime Override Works

Your code does something clever — it doesn't rely on the assistant's fixed config. Instead, it **overrides** the system prompt and first message at call time:

```typescript
// From useVapiInterview.ts
const overrides = {
  firstMessage: `Hi — I'm your interviewer today for the ${interview.role} 
    ${interview.type} session at ${interview.difficulty} level...`,
  model: {
    provider: "google",
    model: "gemini-2.5-flash",
    messages: [{ role: "system", content: systemPrompt }],
    // ↑ This systemPrompt contains the actual questions from Gemini
  },
};

void client.start(assistantId, overrides);
```

This means the dashboard assistant is a **shell** — it provides the voice, transcriber, and infrastructure, while your app injects the actual interview content at runtime.

---

## Troubleshooting

| Issue | Solution |
|-------|----------|
| "Missing NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPI_WEB_TOKEN" error | Check `.env.local` has the correct public key. Restart dev server. |
| "Missing NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPI_ASSISTANT_ID" error | You haven't replaced the placeholder with your real assistant ID. |
| Start button is disabled | `isVapiConfigured()` returns false — both env vars must be set. |
| Call starts but no audio | Allow microphone permission. Check browser console for WebRTC errors. |
| "Failed to connect" | Your Vapi account may have run out of free credits. Check dashboard billing. |
| Voice agent speaks but doesn't ask the right questions | That's expected for now — the system prompt override should fix this. Check console for errors in `vapi.start()`. |
| Transcript is empty | Make sure the transcriber is configured (Deepgram nova-2). Check if `message` events fire in console. |

---

## Summary Checklist

- [ ] Vapi account created at [dashboard.vapi.ai](https://dashboard.vapi.ai)
- [ ] Public API key confirmed in `.env.local` (`NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPI_WEB_TOKEN`)
- [ ] Assistant created on dashboard (Blank template, Google/Gemini model)
- [ ] System prompt set (default/fallback)
- [ ] First message set
- [ ] Voice configured (default or ElevenLabs)
- [ ] Assistant ID copied
- [ ] `NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPI_ASSISTANT_ID` updated in `.env.local`
- [ ] Dev server restarted
- [ ] Tested voice interview flow end-to-end
